  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on computing mean differences from a reference point (zero), weighting each outcome (relative to reference) by its probability. This is effectively computing expected value relative to zero — i.e., just the expected value. Both lotteries have the same expected value (1500), so the reason would be indifferent. The individual, however, does not mention or appear to compute expected values or aggregate outcomes. Instead, they focus on the 25% chance of winning and compare the top outcomes under that shared probability. They prefer A because it offers a higher outcome (6000 vs. 4000) under the same 25% chance, ignoring the additional 25% chance in B for 2000. This reflects a 'priority heuristic' or 'maximizing the best outcome under shared minimum chance' rather than averaging all differences. The individual does not consider all outcome deviations from zero with their probabilities, nor do they compute a mean difference. Their justification is based on partial probability matching and risk-seeking in the domain of rare gains, not on the rule of the decision reason. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
